#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546209 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $B(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$; $C(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546214 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $B(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $C(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546971 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a-b?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=7 \\\\ 3a+6b &=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the first equation is already solved for $a:$\n\n$$\na=7\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=7$ into $3a+6b=15,$ then we can find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+6b & =15 \\\\ 3(7)+6b & =15 \\\\ 6b & =-6 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=7,\\,b=-1.$ So,\n\n$$\na-b=7-(-1)=7+1=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546991 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2b &=6a+14 \\\\ 5b+25 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2b &=6a+14 && \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 5b+25 &=0. && \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2),$ we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b+25 & =0 \\\\ 5b & =-25 \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-5$ into equation $(1)$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-5) & =6a+14 \\\\ -10 & =6a+14 \\\\ -10-14 & =6a \\\\ 6a & =-24 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=-5.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=-4+(-5)=-4-5=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547012 :question/problem "![](images/87/873c7f6b6e036f592f693bf2d20e1980b890f888d08af40b775cb77deba07a76.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the parallelogram $ABCD?$" :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{BD}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ABD$ (see the illustration).\n\n![](images/75/7572dac85d8e6029b52d199bdfcb87b4a92ffafc008fe999c10848153f9f67d8.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ABD,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(10)(6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{38}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{38}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈18.470,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ABD} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 18.470 \\\\  & =36.940.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $37.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547025 :question/problem "![](images/24/243e5fd1911db6fc7dc940a16082c7369c63f913d78d9e18324d977540a310cb.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$" :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{BD}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△CBD$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/da/da5c25d9e1e54a79b8e2c8532d4571dd42ccd4e6e6a3fa69aa1fc9075c19b39d.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△CBD,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{CBD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(21)(12)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{66}^{∘} \\\\  & =126\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{66}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈115.107,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{CBD} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 115.107 \\\\  & =230.214.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $230.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547048 :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8cd5ca7760d21ac5bd86cfb90486ccde755f4aba831808889a20e1ad467eaf9.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ab\\operatorname{sin}⁡C,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠C$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =CA=8, \\\\ a & =CB=10, \\\\ m∠A & ={43}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{43}^{∘}=0.681\\,998,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{43}^{∘} \\\\  & =40\\cdot (0.681\\,998) \\\\  & ≈27.28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547053 :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b922282db5fce22af7df867b36545077f7d609dc83ef97abf46d5f9814cbc3c.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest integer**, the area of $△ABC$ is{{field-1}} ${\\text{mi}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=36, \\\\ b & =AC=36, \\\\ m∠A & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}=0.939\\,693,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 36\\cdot 36\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =648\\cdot (0.939\\,693) \\\\  & ≈609\\,{\\text{mi}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest integer**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547072 :question/problem "![](images/16/160b47689a56ef98c0a0d6b2df6dcf838857ba840ce017ac0707366304f68f83.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $112\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the area of $△BCD=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 24\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘} \\\\  & =192\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘} \\\\  & =157.277… \\\\  & ≈157\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =157-112 \\\\  & =45\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547082 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca15b4523cd12bdfb4661624eba58387e15ebef6e228c1740b4582ba6af556a9.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $175{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 27\\cdot 30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{35}^{∘} \\\\  & =405\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{35}^{∘} \\\\  & =232.298… \\\\  & ≈232{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =232-175 \\\\  & =57\\,{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548021 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }$ for $-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1+{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\theta +{e}^{\\theta })^{2}+(1+{e}^{\\theta })^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta {e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }+1+2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2{e}^{\\theta }(\\theta +1)+2{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548195 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y}$ at the point $A(2,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨4,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8 x y + 2 e^{y},2 x \\left(2 x + e^{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =⟨8 (2) (0) + 2 e^{(0)},4 (2)^{2} + 2 (2) e^{(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,0) & =∇f(2,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{24 \\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548201 :question/problem "![](images/07/0760173ffa9ede7dc926ff42ddc2a2474433408c2320bc6e37f7dc95ffde2dd1.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(- \\frac{9}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and the point $X(-7,3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-7-\\left(- \\frac{9}{2}\\right))}^{2}+{(3-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{9}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548202 :question/problem "![](images/6d/6d31b63333aa977b888d7941ac1195bd214201c20606621bb61eb82c81ee1258.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-1,0)$ and the point $X(-9,-6)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-9-(-1))}^{2}+{(-6-0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(10)^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+y^{2} & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548211 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0ed7e0b3302ef2debc3e5662c51b7e10aa5ca41c55aabdde16f89e78181a96e.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})$ and the point $X(-4,-1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-4-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right))}^{2}+{(-1-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{2})^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{49}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548216 :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1a5a13794db501173c82dd4a7e4ea56af839c6be6e859fbcfd2a35e0a952ec4.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and the point $X(9,0)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(9-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+{(0-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{9}{2}}^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{41}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548221 :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b23d8724583dcb7c76b44a8d403f1d496901a947978bb5b4682870f9c8bc34d.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(- \\frac{5}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})$ and the point $X(0,-3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(0-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right))}^{2}+{(-3-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548222 :question/problem "![](images/9a/9ae602ac0a1240a9096f0d60b031b4aaa3c670d6c873fa0b49940c15b4e9b8c4.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-4,0)$ and the point $X(-8,3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-8-(-4))}^{2}+{(3-0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(5)^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+y^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548224 :question/problem "![](images/61/61afb933b90d96707e44321590837dc4abbfb520ccdc50c3d77ebd930181b9ff.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-5,0)$ and the point $X(-11,8)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-11-(-5))}^{2}+{(8-0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-5))^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(10)^{2} \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+y^{2} & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548228 :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2f1cdddf07f324c01491e359f347484f0feb69e471aaba93e1f410f7584df90.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})$ and the point $X(-8,-3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-8-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))}^{2}+{(-3-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{9}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{41}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548243 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc322a5919f391bab61e491e555096b60697b9c17f2a88851348a552470206a4.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(5,0)$ and the point $X(11,8)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(11-5)}^{2}+{(8-0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(10)^{2} \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+y^{2} & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548261 :question/problem "![](images/14/14636b213a29b8ed188cb8020776cca59deb42ffa3daffc8df7f8b757c4c0fd3.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-3,0)$ and the point $X(1,-3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-3))}^{2}+{(-3-0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(5)^{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+y^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548280 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 9\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 9)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(81 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=81 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548282 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $x - 2\\leq y\\leq 0$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=4 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle|_{z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(3 y\\right)\\left(2 x\\right)-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(4 y\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 6 x y)+(12 x y)+(3)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/667d3cc98a0951d2c6fa4be3684d0ac37c6c7307d2f38f56c9444593492e25f5.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548301 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2 - x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle|_{z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16\\right)\\left(- 2 x\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 2 x \\left(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} + 16\\right))+(0)+(0)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef88b67d11b3848190625d9329f220d0c213cb8252bd1d0de22ba8de035678b.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548302 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 6 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x + 18)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[18 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(18 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=18 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548305 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 x - 4\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 y + 4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle|_{z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} - 4 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 x - 4\\right)-\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 y + 4\\right)+\\left(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 x - 4\\right))+(\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 y + 4\\right))+(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[- 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) - 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) + 32 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(- 16 \\pi r^{3} + 16 r^{2} - 64 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(16 r^{2}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(16 \\pi r \\left(- r^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 \\pi r^{4} - 32 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(- 288 \\pi\\right)+\\left(- 324 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=- 612 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548306 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x - y + 3$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$ The circulation is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2}\\int_0^{2}\n$$ {{field-1}} $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x - y + 3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle|_{z=- x - y + 3}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x y\\right)\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(4 y\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((4 x y)+(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)+(4 y)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1f/1f3f8869870631489863fa51c3b3297626962e265c43938fb1fa10273ec8e677.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548310 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=2}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 4)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(36 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=36 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548320 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=4$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(4\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(4\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=4}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 4 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 16\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 16)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[16 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(144 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=144 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548324 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=2 x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2 x.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x\\right)=2\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle|_{z=2 x}\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(0\\right)\\left(2\\right)-\\left(4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[0\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548329 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=1.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=1}\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 1)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[\\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(9 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=9 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549196 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + 2 y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 2 y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}9\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =9{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549198 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + y^{2} + 1$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-2\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y^{2} + 1)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y^{2} + 1)=2 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\left(x + y^{2} + 1\\right)-x+y^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(2 y\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{4 y^{2} + 2}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{2\\left(2 y^{2} + 1\\right)}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}}\\sqrt{2}\\cancel{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}4 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(-2\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\\\ & =16 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549208 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + 2 y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + 2 y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3 \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\left(1-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =6 \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549211 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}1\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =2 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549224 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + 2 y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 2 y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}1\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549253 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 8 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-8 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-8\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-8\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549258 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2\\left(xy\\right) \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=2\\left(xy\\right),\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2\\left(y\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2\\left(x\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScaling the three derivative rows by $2, 1, 1$ multiplies the determinant by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\left(y\\right) & 2\\left(x\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\left(y\\begin{vmatrix}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}-x\\begin{vmatrix}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\  & =2\\left(y(xz-0)-x(0-yz)\\right)\\\\  & =2\\left(xyz+xyz\\right)\\\\  & =4 x y z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(2xyz\\right) & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)vw}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4 \\sqrt{u v w}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549263 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-2 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-2\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
